% ============================================================================
% mirror-focal-length-sign — for the two mirror types, the SIGN convention
% the SAME already-cited OpenStax sentence states for its focal length
% (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `lens-types.adj`
% (`optic_action(optic, action)`, whether each of four basic optical
% elements converges or diverges parallel light — convex_lens →
% converges_light, concave_lens → diverges_light, concave_mirror →
% converges_light, convex_mirror → diverges_light). That table's own
% header already quotes, verbatim, an OpenStax sentence for EACH of the
% two mirror rows that states the SIGN of the mirror's focal length — a
% fact the action-only schema had no room for:
%
%   concave_mirror → positive
%     "The focal length f of a concave mirror is positive, since it is a
%      converging mirror"
%
%   convex_mirror → negative
%     "The focal length and power of a convex mirror are negative, since
%      it is a diverging mirror."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? mirror_focal_length_sign(concave_mirror, $Sign)   % positive
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table): each
% `row` lowers to a ground relation `mirror_focal_length_sign(mirror,
% sign)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD
% binding query — the engine returns the sign WITH its source, on the
% CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on anything that is not one of
% the two mirror types. `convex_lens` and `concave_lens` are deliberately
% NOT rows here: their own already-quoted OpenStax sentences describe how
% the rays behave (intersect / diverge) but never state a sign convention
% for the lens's focal length, so this sibling is HONESTLY NARROWER than
% its parent — it grounds exactly the two mirror types and abstains on
% the two lens types, or any other word, rather than inventing a sign
% neither lens quote states. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a sign
% and recall which mirror has it:
%
%     ? mirror_focal_length_sign($Mirror, negative)   % convex_mirror
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME two OpenStax sentences
% already quoted inside `lens-types.adj`'s own header (its
% `concave_mirror`/`convex_mirror` rows), from the SAME OpenStax College
% Physics chapter, via Physics LibreTexts, that table already cites — no
% new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding the sign-convention half
% of each already-verified quote. An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance
% envelope (per `lens-types.adj`'s own convention), so `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the concave_mirror span; the other row's own span is
% listed above for independent auditability. `trust consensus` — the same
% tier `lens-types.adj` already carries (OpenStax via Physics LibreTexts,
% a widely used teaching resource, not a primary government/standards
% body).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table mirror_focal_length_sign {
    columns mirror, sign

    row (concave_mirror, positive)   % OpenStax: "The focal length f of a concave mirror is positive, since it is a converging mirror"
    row (convex_mirror, negative)    % OpenStax: "The focal length and power of a convex mirror are negative, since it is a diverging mirror."

    source "The focal length f of a concave mirror is positive, since it is a converging mirror"
    locator "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/College_Physics/College_Physics_1e_(OpenStax)/25:_Geometric_Optics/25.07:_Image_Formation_by_Mirrors"
    trust consensus

    cites "The focal length and power of a convex mirror are negative, since it is a diverging mirror." locator "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/College_Physics/College_Physics_1e_(OpenStax)/25:_Geometric_Optics/25.07:_Image_Formation_by_Mirrors"
}
